<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lokono</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lokono people, Arawaks of the Guianas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lokono, often called Arawaks, are an Indigenous people of the coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guianas">Guianas</a> who speak an Arawakan language. They traded and fought with European colonists in the seventeenth century, and in 1686 Governor Van Aerssen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> made peace with them and the Kalina. Lokono communities live today in Suriname, Guyana, French Guiana and Venezuela.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Lokono comes from loko, people, and the plural suffix no. The Lokono language gave its name to the Arawakan family, which may have developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> valley and spread with its speakers until it was the most widespread language family of the region at the time of European contact. The Lokono settled the coasts and river valleys of what are now Guyana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and French Guiana, as well as Barbados and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>. In 1989 the Lokono linguist John Peter Bennett wrote that Arawak was not originally a Lokono word but one they had adopted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>While the Spanish quickly colonised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> islands, the Lokono and other mainland peoples held out much longer, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> could not subdue them through the 16th century. As other European powers pressed in early in the 17th century, the Lokono allied with Spain against the Kalina, who sided with the English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and then traded with the Europeans to their profit. In 1627 English colonists persuaded a Lokono family to move to Barbados to help with farming. In the 18th century Dutch colonists hired Lokono as fishermen and salt miners, though they were not enslaved, and in the 19th century they worked under the English.</p><p>The end of the plantation economy and other changes of the early 19th century hurt the Lokono, and their numbers fell. In the 20th century they added the sale of fish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and migrant labour to their farming, and their population has grown again to about 10,000, most of them in Guyana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Lokono were historically matrilineal farming societies. Their own religion respects the spirits of nature, with whom healers communicate to cure people and give advice; in the 18th century Jeptha, a Lokono, translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and German hymns into the language with the help of two boys. Many Lokono have married Afro-Guyanese, whose children are accepted as Amerindian, and many have taken on Afro-Guyanese and Indo-Guyanese ways while working to keep their language alive, with a 10-month language programme for children in Guyanese schools. Besides Lokono they speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> languages and English.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lokono” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Van Aerssen, the first governor under the Society, made peace with the Indigenous Kalina and <b>Lokono peoples</b> in 1686 and imposed discipline on planters and soldiers, until mutinous soldiers killed him in 1688.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
